# Component-Aware Naming Convention with BEM & data-testid

## Overview

Modern web applications use build tools that mangle class names (CSS Modules, styled-components, Tailwind purging). This breaks external tooling like Fullstory, Playwright, and AI agents. The most robust solution is to combine a stable **BEM-based class naming strategy** with a dedicated `data-testid` attribute for tooling.

**The Solution**: Use BEM class names that encode component structure, and add a single `data-testid` attribute per interactive element. This approach is lightweight, framework-agnostic, and works without build-time plugins.

---

## The Problem

```html
<!-- What renders -->
<button class="Button_primaryButton__x7Ks2">Add to Cart</button>
<!-- This hash changes every build -->
```

**Common approaches (and why they fall short):**
- ❌ Pure class-name conventions cannot survive CSS Module hashing.
- ❌ `data-component` / `data-element` attributes pollute the DOM with multiple extra attributes.
- ❌ `aria-*` attributes are for accessibility, not automation targeting.

**The Better Way**: Use a single `data-testid` attribute per component that combines component name and element purpose. This is already supported by Cypress, Playwright, and Fullstory.

---

## Why This Matters for AI Agents (CUA)

Computer User Agents need stable identifiers:

```
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ❌ Brittle: AI sees random classes                        │
│  ✅ Stable: AI sees data-testid="ProductCard__add-to-cart" │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

A single `data-testid` is easier for AI to parse than multiple data attributes.

---

## The Solution: BEM + data-testid

### Naming Convention

Use a concatenated BEM-style pattern:

```
{component}__{element}--{modifier}
```

- Component: PascalCase matching file name.
- Element: kebab-case describing purpose.
- Modifier: optional, for variants.

**Examples:**
| Element | data-testid |
|---------|-------------|
| Add to Cart button in ProductCard | `ProductCard__add-to-cart` |
| Price display in ProductCard | `ProductCard__price-display` |
| Search input in Header | `Header__search-input` |
| Submit button in CheckoutForm | `CheckoutForm__submit--disabled` |

### Implementation in React (TypeScript)

```tsx
// ProductCard.tsx
interface ProductCardProps {
  product: Product;
  onAddToCart: (product: Product) => void;
}

function ProductCard({ product, onAddToCart }: ProductCardProps) {
  return (
    <div className="product-card" data-testid="ProductCard__card">
      <img
        src={product.image}
        alt={product.name}
        className="product-card__image"
        data-testid="ProductCard__product-image"
      />
      <h3 className="product-card__name" data-testid="ProductCard__product-name">
        {product.name}
      </h3>
      <span className="product-card__price" data-testid="ProductCard__price-display">
        ${product.price}
      </span>
      <button
        className="product-card__add-btn"
        data-testid="ProductCard__add-to-cart"
        onClick={() => onAddToCart(product)}
      >
        Add to Cart
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Why this works:**
- `data-testid` is never hashed or stripped by default.
- Single attribute reduces DOM pollution.
- Standardized pattern works with all test runners.

### TypeScript Type Safety

Create a helper to enforce test IDs:

```typescript
// testIds.ts
type TestId<T extends string> = `${T}__${string}`;

function createTestId<T extends string>(component: T) {
  return {
    root: `${component}__card` as TestId<T>,
    element: (element: string) => `${component}__${element}` as TestId<T>,
  };
}

// Usage
const testId = createTestId('ProductCard');
<button data-testid={testId.element('add-to-cart')}>Add</button>
```

